# /learn

Capture **one** thing, immediately, while it is still exact.

The end-of-session sweep loses nuance: by then you remember *that* you were corrected, not the
sentence that corrected you. This command exists so capture costs seconds and can happen inline.

```bash
MEM="{{MEM}}"     # the corpus; filled in at install time
```

## Usage

```
/learn                          # infer from the last exchange
/learn {{USER}} wants X, not Y
/learn --type reference "the service listens on 23306, not 3306"
```

## Trigger — run this without being asked

- {{USER}} **corrects** you, or says "no", "actually", "next time", "always", "never", "I told you"
- {{USER}} states a **preference** about how work should be done
- You hit a **non-obvious fact** that cost real effort to establish — an error's true cause, an
  undocumented port, a flag that silently does nothing
- {{USER}} says **"remember this"**

Do **not** run it for things the repo already records: code structure, git history, a fix that
lives in a commit, anything in project instructions. If {{USER}} asks you to remember one of
those, ask what was non-obvious about it and save *that* instead.

## Steps

### 1. Classify

| Type | Goes in | Test |
|---|---|---|
| `feedback` | `feedback/` | a rule about **how I should work** |
| `project` | `project/` | state of **ongoing work** not derivable from code or git |
| `reference` | `reference/` | a **pointer or fact** about an external system or tool |

Interaction narrative is *not* a durable memory — that belongs in the session log.

### 2. Reconcile against what exists — RUN THE TOOL, do not eyeball it

This step has a **measured failure rate**. Three separate memories describing one identical
environment fact were written inside a **four-hour window, by one person**, because the step was
prose and prose is treated as advisory.

So it is not prose any more. Run it:

```bash
python3 engine/find-similar.py --desc "<the description you were about to write>" <key> <nouns>
```

It ranks existing memories by description overlap and similarity, flags likely duplicates, and
prints nothing you can skim past. A memory whose **description** already covers the fact is the
one retrieval will surface *instead of* your new file — which is why description matches outrank
body matches in its scoring.

Then record a verdict against every flagged candidate:

| Verdict | When | Action |
|---|---|---|
| **NOOP** | an existing memory already says this | write nothing; say which file covers it |
| **UPDATE** | an existing memory is right but incomplete or now partly wrong | edit that file in place; do not create a sibling |
| **ADD** | genuinely new, no overlapping memory | write the new file |
| **SUPERSEDE** | an existing memory is now **wrong**, not merely stale | correct it in place; note what changed and when |

Two memories may coexist on one topic only if they answer **different questions**. "It is about a
different ticket" is not a different question — that is exactly how one ticket acquired two
`project/` memories, two days apart, carrying contradictory status.

**Default to UPDATE.** Fewer, richer, current memories beat a larger corpus retrieval cannot
disambiguate. When torn between ADD and UPDATE, choose UPDATE.

### 3. Write it — through the helper, not the Write tool

```bash
engine/mem-write.sh <feedback|project|reference> <kebab-slug> <<'EOF'
---
name: <kebab-slug>
description: <one line — this is what gets injected and indexed, so make it a routing signal>
metadata:
  type: feedback | project | reference
  scope: global | domain          # feedback only — see below; omit and it means global
---

<the fact, stated so it is actionable a month from now>

**Why:** <the incident that produced it>
**How to apply:** <what to do differently next time>
EOF
```

**Use the helper. The Write and Edit tools cannot write here.** The corpus lives inside the Claude
Code config directory so the whole directory stays one portable unit, and Claude Code treats any path
inside a `.claude` directory as a sensitive file that those tools refuse per file — an allow rule does
not override it. Bash writes there fine, which is what the helper is.

The helper also **enforces** what the template above only shows, and refuses the write otherwise:

| It rejects | Because |
|---|---|
| a missing or very short `description:` | it is the routing signal; a weak one makes the memory unfindable |
| `name:` not matching the filename | the index keys on `name:`, so a mismatch makes the entry point nowhere |
| a missing `metadata.type:` | it decides which tier the memory lands in |
| a `metadata.scope:` that is not `global` or `domain` | a typo would silently take the default and seat a domain rule in every context window |
| `feedback` or `project` with no **Why:** / **How to apply:** | a rule without its incident is unarguable later, and one without an action is unusable |
| an existing file, unless you pass `--update` | that is step 2's verdict, enforced |

**Feedback memories choose a tier.** `MEMORY.md` is injected into every context window and
`MEMORY-CATALOG.md` is read on demand, so a `feedback` memory declares which one it belongs in:

- `scope: global` — it changes behaviour on **any** task, whatever the repo, language or tool.
- `scope: domain` — it is bound to one of those. It goes to the catalog, where `/recall` still finds it.

The test that decides it: *would I want this in front of me before I know what the task is?* Consent
and epistemic rules usually pass; craft advice about one artifact usually does not. Omitting the
field means `global`, because defaulting the other way would empty the always-on tier the first time
an older corpus is rebuilt — but **omitting it is not the same as choosing it**. Decide, then write.

An always-on tier that only ever grows stops being delivered at all: past an undocumented size
ceiling the harness silently swaps the whole index for a file preview, and nothing errors. That is
what `RAEMEMBERIT_ALWAYS_ON_BUDGET` and the SessionStart tripwire exist for, and neither of them can
make this judgement for you.

A slug filename, **not** a dated one — dates are for interaction logs. Convert relative dates in the
body to absolute; "last Tuesday" is unreadable in a month. Link related memories with `[[slug]]`,
liberally: a link to a memory that does not exist yet marks something worth writing later, not an
error.

### 4. The corpus refuses a duplicate on its own

The helper rebuilds both indexes and runs the duplicate gate for you. It **exits 3** if the write you
just made left a near-duplicate behind, naming the pair.

That is the point: it turns this corpus's most common defect from something an audit discovers months
later into something the write itself refuses to leave quietly. If it fires, go back to step 2 and
take UPDATE — do not talk yourself into ADD to get past the check. The file is still on disk so you
can merge it rather than retype it.

### 5. Confirm in one line

State the path and the type. Do not narrate the whole file back.

